ii 
 
Preface 
It is a popular saying that population is not only about total figures but more importantly about its distribution across age  and sex.  
These two variables determine the structure and composition of the population including its potenti al to grow.  The main mission of a 
population census is to tell the story of the living conditions of the people at a particular time and give insight into how these conditions 
can be ameliorated.  Definitely, there is no way the total story can be told if  the age and sex structure of the population is not taken into 
consideration. 
It is in recognition of this fact that the National Population Commission not only collected variables on the age and sex of respondents 
during the 2006 Population and Housing Ce nsus as it is consistent with global practices but also ensured that these data have been 
separately processed, analysed a s being disseminated through this publication.  The volume presents the Population Distribution of 
Nigeria by Age and Sex as captured during the 2006 Census.  The data has been presented for each of the states and the Federal Capital 
Territory and the Local Government Areas of the Federation in five year age groups. 
It is hoped that the publication of this volume will enhance the usefulness of the 2006 Census data for planning purposes.  The data can 
serve as the nation’s indispensable guide in the march towards sustainable development.  The essence of development is to make concrete 
improvement in the living conditions of the people.  Since the people have different needs and potentials across sex and age divides, 
planning for development must therefore be gender sensitive and reflect the various needs of the different age groups in the society.  This 
is the best way any development can be for all. 
It is our hope that planners in all sectors of our nation life will avail themselves of the rare insights offered into Nigeri a’s population 
structure by this publication and similar one s that have been produced under the 2006 Census Data Dissemination series in order to 
make development planning more responsive. 
The Commission is grateful to individuals, state and local governments as well as the development partners who all contributed to the 
successful conduct of the 2006 Census. 
 
Chief Samu’ila Danko Makama, CON 
Chairman 
National Population Commission 
  
iii 
 
Notes 
 
This section provides information on the arrangement of this publication and explanation of some concepts used. 
This volume presents data on the population distribution by age and sex canvassed in the 2006 Census.  It 
contains tables on the Distribution of Population by Age and Sex for all  36 states and the FCT arranged in 
alphabetical order.  Under each state, the corresponding tables for the Local Government Areas are presented. 
Find below explanations of some of the concepts used in this publication: 
Sex:  This is the gender of the respondents and it can either be male or female 
Age:  This is calculated in completed year or age at last birthday. 
 
  
